WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.402
On Day 20 you wired up hello-devops and Redis by hand.

2
00:00:06.402 --> 00:00:11.887
A network, a volume, and two long docker run commands, typed in the right order.

3
00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:13.400
That is imperative.

4
00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:15.178
You tell Docker each step.

5
00:00:15.178 --> 00:00:18.051
It works, and nobody wants to do it twice.

6
00:00:18.867 --> 00:00:21.015
Docker Compose is declarative.

7
00:00:21.015 --> 00:00:25.024
You describe the finished app in one file, compose.yaml.

8
00:00:25.233 --> 00:00:32.406
It has one entry per service: one for web, built from your Dockerfile, and one for Redis, from a ready-made image.

9
00:00:32.406 --> 00:00:34.356
And it declares a named volume.

10
00:00:34.567 --> 00:00:36.688
Then you run docker compose up.

11
00:00:36.688 --> 00:00:38.399
Compose is only a client.

12
00:00:38.399 --> 00:00:44.763
It turns the file into the same requests to Docker Engine that your docker run commands made.

13
00:00:46.567 --> 00:00:48.037
It works out the steps.

14
00:00:48.037 --> 00:00:53.215
First the things the containers need: the volume, and a network for this project.

15
00:00:53.433 --> 00:00:58.155
Every name starts with the project, and the project is named after the folder.

16
00:00:58.767 --> 00:01:03.036
Then the Redis container, with the volume mounted at slash data.

17
00:01:03.036 --> 00:01:08.039
The -1 is a counter, because Compose can run several copies of one service.

18
00:01:08.267 --> 00:01:09.248
Now the catch.

19
00:01:09.248 --> 00:01:12.611
depends_on makes Compose start Redis before web.

20
00:01:12.833 --> 00:01:17.165
In its short form, it only waits until the Redis container is running.

21
00:01:17.165 --> 00:01:21.807
But a database can need several more seconds before it accepts connections.

22
00:01:22.033 --> 00:01:24.259
Running is not the same as ready.

23
00:01:24.467 --> 00:01:26.581
A healthcheck closes that gap.

24
00:01:26.581 --> 00:01:31.091
It is a command Docker runs inside the container, on a schedule.

25
00:01:31.091 --> 00:01:33.980
Here, redis-cli ping, every five seconds.

26
00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:36.216
Exit code zero means healthy.

27
00:01:36.216 --> 00:01:39.205
Until a check passes, Redis shows starting.

28
00:01:39.433 --> 00:01:43.771
In the lesson's run, Redis showed healthy after 5.8 seconds.

29
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:50.131
The long form of depends_on, with condition service_healthy, waits for exactly that.

30
00:01:50.131 --> 00:01:53.635
Only now does Compose start web, at 6.0 seconds.

31
00:01:54.267 --> 00:01:59.567
Every service is on the project network, so service names work as DNS names.

32
00:01:59.567 --> 00:02:01.939
Web finds Redis by the name redis.

33
00:02:02.167 --> 00:02:04.984
Only port 8000 is published to your Mac.

34
00:02:04.984 --> 00:02:08.857
Redis has no ports entry, because only web talks to it.

35
00:02:10.167 --> 00:02:13.050
But how does Compose know what is already there?

36
00:02:13.050 --> 00:02:18.756
It labels every container with its project, its service, and a hash of that service's settings.

37
00:02:18.967 --> 00:02:22.195
Say you change one setting of web, and run up again.

38
00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:25.141
Compose compares the labels with the file.

39
00:02:25.141 --> 00:02:27.425
Redis matches, so it keeps running.

40
00:02:27.425 --> 00:02:28.991
Web's hash is different.

41
00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:31.255
So only web is recreated.

42
00:02:31.867 --> 00:02:35.713
docker compose down removes the containers, and the network.

43
00:02:35.933 --> 00:02:37.419
It keeps named volumes.

44
00:02:37.419 --> 00:02:41.877
So the next up finds your Redis data, and the visit count carries on.

45
00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:47.312
docker compose down -v also deletes the volumes, and your data with them.

46
00:02:47.933 --> 00:02:50.258
compose.yaml describes the whole app.

47
00:02:50.258 --> 00:02:57.483
docker compose up makes the steps, in the right order, and with a healthcheck it waits for ready, not just running.
